problem_statement.body:
The MySQL dialect rejects valid `SET ROLE` statements, including `SET ROLE DEFAULT;`, `SET ROLE NONE;`, and `SET ROLE ALL;`. Lists of named roles and `ALL EXCEPT` also produce an unparsable section.

I reproduced all eight variants below on current main using `Linter(dialect="mysql").parse_string(...)`:

```sql
SET ROLE DEFAULT;
SET ROLE NONE;
SET ROLE ALL;
SET ROLE 'reader';
SET ROLE 'reader', 'writer';
SET ROLE 'reader'@'localhost';
SET ROLE ALL EXCEPT 'writer';
SET ROLE ALL EXCEPT 'reader'@'localhost', 'writer';
```

These forms are documented in the [MySQL 8.4 SET ROLE reference](https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/set-role.html). The existing `SET DEFAULT ROLE ... TO ...` support covers a different statement.

I plan to add a focused MySQL statement segment reusing `RoleReferenceSegment`, with positive SQL/YAML parser fixtures, then run MySQL/MariaDB regression checks and the required quality checks. This adds support for valid SQL without tightening existing grammar.

AI assistance: Codex helped inspect the grammar and reproduce the parse failures.

A member `@offset` (or `@align`) inside a struct that is used as a member of a constant buffer is lost on SPIR-V, HLSL and GLSL output. Each target places the nested member at its natural layout offset, so the shader reads other bytes than the host wrote, and the validators still pass.

Reproducer (`nested.cgl`):

```
shader Probe {
    struct Inner { float s; vec4 v @offset(32); };
    cbuffer C { Inner n; };
    compute {
        layout(local_size_x = 1, local_size_y = 1, local_size_z = 1) in;
        buffer float* outp;
        void main() {
            uint i = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;
            outp[i] = n.s + n.v.y;
        }
    }
}
```

Observed at ba41e50d9:

| Target | Output for `Inner.v` |
|---|---|
| vulkan | `OpMemberDecorate %Inner_std140_9 1 Offset 16` |
| directx | `float4 v : offset;` (the attribute is emitted as a semantic named `offset`) |
| opengl | `vec4 v;` |
| metal | `float4 v [[offset]];` (not valid MSL) |
| slang | `float4 v : offset;` |

Expected: each target either places `v` at byte 32 of `Inner` or raises a typed `MemberPlacementError`. SPIR-V can write `Offset 32` on the nested struct type. GLSL `layout(offset)` and HLSL `packoffset` do not reach members of a nested struct, so those targets need explicit padding members or a refusal.

Top-level members (`cbuffer C { float s; vec4 v @offset(32); }`) are planned or refused by the new `crosstl/translator/semantics/member_placements.py` (PR for #4196); nested structs need the same treatment.

